WEBVTT

00:00.310 --> 00:02.500
- [Instructor] Erin was
asked to find if f of x

00:02.500 --> 00:05.620
is equal to x squared
minus one to the 2/3 power

00:05.620 --> 00:07.890
has a relative maximum.

00:07.890 --> 00:09.820
This is her solution.

00:09.820 --> 00:12.333
And then they give us her
steps, and at the end they say,

00:12.333 --> 00:13.970
is Erin's work correct?

00:13.970 --> 00:16.230
If not, what's her mistake?

00:16.230 --> 00:17.790
So pause this video and see if you can

00:17.790 --> 00:18.630
figure it out yourself.

00:18.630 --> 00:20.270
Is Erin correct, or did
you she make a mistake,

00:20.270 --> 00:21.510
and where was that mistake?

00:21.510 --> 00:23.400
All right, now let's just do it together.

00:23.400 --> 00:25.190
So she says that this is the derivative.

00:25.190 --> 00:28.470
I'm just going to reevaluate
here to the right of her work.

00:28.470 --> 00:30.900
So let's see, f prime
of x is just going to be

00:30.900 --> 00:31.980
the chain rule.

00:31.980 --> 00:33.420
I'm gonna take the
derivative of the outside

00:33.420 --> 00:34.510
with respect to the inside.

00:34.510 --> 00:38.980
So this is going to be 2/3
times x squared minus one

00:38.980 --> 00:43.350
to the 2/3 minus one, so
to the negative 1/3 power,

00:43.350 --> 00:46.410
times the derivative of the
inside with respect to x.

00:46.410 --> 00:48.614
So the derivative of x squared
minus one with respect to x

00:48.614 --> 00:51.310
is two x.

00:51.310 --> 00:52.143
(siren ringing)

00:52.143 --> 00:54.278
There's a fire hydrant, a fire (laughing),

00:54.278 --> 00:55.990
not a hydrant, that
would be a noisy hydrant.

00:55.990 --> 00:58.440
There's a fire truck outside, but okay,

00:58.440 --> 00:59.780
I think it's passed.

00:59.780 --> 01:02.040
But this looks like what
she got for the derivative.

01:02.040 --> 01:05.230
Because if you multiply two times two x,

01:05.230 --> 01:07.260
you do indeed get four x.

01:07.260 --> 01:10.510
You have this three right
over here in the denominator.

01:10.510 --> 01:13.150
And x squared minus one
to the negative 1/3,

01:13.150 --> 01:15.155
that's the same thing
as x squared minus one

01:15.155 --> 01:16.790
to the 1/3 in the denominator,

01:16.790 --> 01:18.410
which is the same thing as the cubed root

01:18.410 --> 01:20.100
of x squared minus one.

01:20.100 --> 01:22.380
So all of this is looking good.

01:22.380 --> 01:24.700
That is indeed the derivative.

01:24.700 --> 01:28.430
Step two, the critical
point is x equals zero.

01:28.430 --> 01:32.480
So let's see, a critical point
is where our first derivative

01:32.480 --> 01:36.340
is either equal to zero
or it is undefined.

01:36.340 --> 01:40.770
And so it does indeed
seem that f prime of zero

01:40.770 --> 01:41.970
is going to be four times zero,

01:41.970 --> 01:45.295
it's gonna be zero over
three times the cubed root

01:45.295 --> 01:48.130
of zero minus one, of negative one.

01:48.130 --> 01:50.540
And so this is three times negative one,

01:50.540 --> 01:51.580
or zero over negative three,

01:51.580 --> 01:53.760
so this is indeed equal to zero.

01:53.760 --> 01:55.090
So this is true.

01:55.090 --> 01:57.970
A critical point is at x equals zero.

01:57.970 --> 02:00.404
But a question is, is this
the only critical point?

02:00.404 --> 02:02.640
Well as we've mentioned, a critical point

02:02.640 --> 02:06.060
is where a function's derivative
is either equal to zero

02:06.060 --> 02:07.650
or it's undefined.

02:07.650 --> 02:10.610
This is the only one where the
derivative's equal to zero,

02:10.610 --> 02:12.270
but can you find some x-values

02:12.270 --> 02:13.970
where the derivative is undefined?

02:13.970 --> 02:15.750
Well what if we make the derivative,

02:15.750 --> 02:18.150
what would make the
denominator of the derivative

02:18.150 --> 02:19.790
equal to zero?

02:19.790 --> 02:21.497
Well if x squared minus
one is equal to zero,

02:21.497 --> 02:23.310
you take the cube root of zero,

02:23.310 --> 02:25.280
you're gonna get zero in the denominator.

02:25.280 --> 02:28.460
So what would make x squared
minus one equal to zero?

02:28.460 --> 02:30.890
Well x is equal to plus or minus one.

02:30.890 --> 02:33.560
These are also critical
points because they make

02:33.560 --> 02:37.043
f prime of x undefined.

02:38.050 --> 02:40.950
So I'm not feeling good about step two.

02:40.950 --> 02:43.620
It is true that a critical
point is x equals zero,

02:43.620 --> 02:46.010
but it is not the only critical point.

02:46.010 --> 02:47.740
So I would put that there.

02:47.740 --> 02:49.150
And the reason why it's important,

02:49.150 --> 02:51.047
you might say, "Well what's
the harm in not noticing

02:51.047 --> 02:52.001
"these other critical points?

02:52.001 --> 02:53.447
"She identified one,

02:53.447 --> 02:55.960
"maybe this is the
relative maximum point."

02:55.960 --> 02:57.790
But as we talked about in other videos,

02:57.790 --> 03:01.100
in order to use the first
derivative test, so to speak,

03:01.100 --> 03:03.710
and find this place where
the first derivative is zero,

03:03.710 --> 03:07.250
in order to test whether it
is a maximum or minimum point,

03:07.250 --> 03:09.790
is you have to sample
values on either side of it

03:09.790 --> 03:11.670
to make sure that you have a change,

03:11.670 --> 03:14.420
a change in sign of the derivative.

03:14.420 --> 03:15.760
But you have to make
sure that when you test

03:15.760 --> 03:17.372
on either side that
you're not going beyond

03:17.372 --> 03:18.610
another critical point.

03:18.610 --> 03:20.330
Because critical points are places

03:20.330 --> 03:22.600
where you can change direction.

03:22.600 --> 03:24.530
And so let's see what
she does in step three

03:24.530 --> 03:25.820
right over here.

03:25.820 --> 03:28.600
Well it is indeed in step
three that's she's testing,

03:28.600 --> 03:30.788
she's trying to test values on either side

03:30.788 --> 03:33.910
of the critical point that she,

03:33.910 --> 03:36.380
that the one critical
point that she identified.

03:36.380 --> 03:39.461
But the problem here, the reason
why this is a little shady,

03:39.461 --> 03:43.250
is this is beyond another critical point

03:43.250 --> 03:44.710
that is less than zero,

03:44.710 --> 03:46.330
and this is beyond, this is greater

03:46.330 --> 03:48.820
than another critical point
that is greater than zero.

03:48.820 --> 03:50.970
This is larger than
the critical point one,

03:50.970 --> 03:54.660
and this is less than the
critical point negative one.

03:54.660 --> 03:58.540
What she should've tried is x equals 0.5

03:58.540 --> 04:00.950
and x equals negative 0.5.

04:00.950 --> 04:02.260
So this is what she shoulda done

04:02.260 --> 04:07.260
is try maybe negative two, negative one,

04:07.500 --> 04:10.410
negative 1/2, zero,

04:10.410 --> 04:14.900
1/2, and then one we know is undefined,

04:14.900 --> 04:16.390
and then positive two.

04:16.390 --> 04:18.240
Because this is a candidate extremum,

04:19.716 --> 04:21.630
this is a candidate extremum,

04:21.630 --> 04:24.200
and this is a candidate
extremum right over here.

04:24.200 --> 04:27.160
And so you wanna see in
which of these situations

04:27.160 --> 04:29.260
you have a sign change of the derivative.

04:29.260 --> 04:30.580
And you just wanna test in the intervals

04:30.580 --> 04:32.590
between the extremum points.

04:32.590 --> 04:36.430
So I would say that really
the main mistake she made

04:36.430 --> 04:38.545
is in step two is not identifying

04:38.545 --> 04:40.833
all of the critical points.
